{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/478551-introduction-to-super-senses-101-or-i-smell-what-you-did-last-summer","id":478551,"title":"Introduction to Super Senses 101, or I Smell What You Did Last Summer","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-12T18:11:16-07:00","id":478551,"title":"Introduction to Super Senses 101, or I Smell What You Did Last Summer","content":"So I've been impressed with the idea of wolf senses since I saw them in a book. I think it's a really nice addition, and the caveat that different forms have different default senses, with the only requirement to switch being letting the Storyteller know, is a nice one that doesn't force the Storyteller to warn players about every possible danger around every possible corner. While still allowing more paranoid players to keep checking.<br /> <br /> I also like the idea that upon your First Change any senses lost are gained, because the idea of someone who was blind or deaf finding a new point of view as a werewolf is a strong one. But that's just an aside.<br /> <br /> Anyways, the show Daredevil has just premiered on Netflix. For a brief introduction, he had toxic material flung into his eye during a good deed as a child and was rendered blind. His other senses compensated, and due to the chemicals, compensated tenfold, giving him effective super senses which he uses to fight crime. There's a lot more to it but that's the important part.<br /> <br /> Throughout the show, Matt Murdock, the Daredevil, uses his senses to do a lot of different things, and I thought it would be interesting to see what specific things they could see doing as a werewolf, and what rolls might be involved.<br /> <br /> Perhaps used the most is his ability to listen to heartbeats. He uses this to pinpoint people, but when dealing with them one on one to detect lies through the increase of heart rate. While that's easy to get a false positive on, do you think you would allow wolf senses to offer bonuses to an attempt to detect a lie?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-12T18:56:12-07:00","id":478592,"title":"","content":"There's a rule in the Storytelling section that gives the idea of successes on Perception rolls adding die to further rolls that take advantage of information gained from it. Seems to be exactly what you're looking for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/717-monghani","name":"Monghani","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar717_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-13T08:43:43-07:00","id":479081,"title":"","content":"There is a lot of information that can be gained through Wolf senses, like knowing:<br /> - If someone is bleeding, or has bled recently.<br /> - If someone is sick, ill or enduring great pain<br /> - If a woman has had her period recently, or if she's pregnant, or if she's breastfeeding.<br /> - If someone is sexually aroused, or afraid, or doubtful, or relaxed, or has been doing physical exercise recently, or if he's had sex that day.<br /> - What brand of cologne he's using, or soap, or shampoo, or tobacco, gum, makeup, nail polish, etc.<br /> - What she ate that day, or if she drank any alcohol.<br /> - What sort of place she's been before, whether it's a hospital, a car, a parking lot, a construction site, a dig, a gym, etc.<br /> - Some medical conditions, like diabetes, some allergies, imminent heart attacks or strokes, etc.<br /> <br /> Having said, there are three things you need to consider before treating a character's wolf senses like a sensor array: Experience, familiarity and physical proximity. There are a huge number of different sensory input and uncountable combinations, and a werewolf may not be familiar with everything. The longest a werewolf has experienced with his wolf senses, the more familiar he is with a an individual and the more physically close he can be, the better. A character might be intimately familiar with a friend or a sex-partner, but when a stranger comes and leaves a scent of cloved cigarettes and a musky, spent sweat the werewolf may not have the necessary context to interpret what his senses are telling him.<br /> <br /> The trick I think is using wolf senses as a narrative tool first, and sensory information second, with a wider gap the more unfamiliar and far away the werewolf is from the sensory stimulus.<br /> <br /> That's my two cents, anyways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-13T16:23:52-07:00","id":479598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zooroos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/478551-introduction-to-super-senses-101-or-i-smell-what-you-did-last-summer?p=479081#post479081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character might be intimately familiar with a friend or a sex-partner, but when a stranger comes and leaves a scent of cloved cigarettes and a musky, spent sweat the werewolf may not have the necessary context to interpret what his senses are telling him.</div> </div> </div> </div>That character would, IMO. That musky scent? same pheromones that you or your partner would emit during sex. Now, if we're talking a virgin, 12 years old werewolf who just changed...<br /> <br /> It'd be harder to for example, realize some car is rigged to explode without any previous exposure to the specific explosives that were placed there, aside from &quot;Your car smells funny&quot;. Even then I'd prop some rolls to determinate it, like wits+science.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}